<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Batch size choice</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Aiming to avoid expensive hyperparameter tuning, we used the XCiT original recipe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
However, in our early experimentations, we observed fluctuations in the training curves of the AF and APS models, which were alleviated by reducing the batch size.
To decide fair batch sizes, we trained the <em id="A4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Nano</em> variants of Baseline, APS, and AF with batch sizes 512 and 1024 and picked, for each method, the configuration that performed best.
The Baseline favored 1024 (top<span id="A4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>1 <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>70.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70.4</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>70.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70.1</annotation></semantics></math> at 512), while APS and AF favored 512 (APS: <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="68.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>68.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">68.7</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="67.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>67.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">67.2</annotation></semantics></math> at 1024; AF: <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>70.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70.4</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>70.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70.1</annotation></semantics></math> at 1024).
We therefore used batch size <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1024</annotation></semantics></math> for the Baseline and <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math> for APS and AF throughout the paper.
Note that the figures above for the AF and APS models are with an AvgPool head.
We applied the same choice to the Nano and Small variants with CA and AFCA heads without further tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
